WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:11.300
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May I then invite the board please to move on to the next item, which is the

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.560
Finance report.  Paul?

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:17.700
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: The report in front of you
sets out the latest financial performance

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.780
data for the commissioning system as at month
11, and indeed, our forecast for the year,

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.990
which draws to a close tomorrow. The numbers
are fully in line with the expectations I

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:31.119
have set out in my last few reports to the
Board, and the time, frankly, to intervene

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:37.370
in financial delivery is pretty much over,
so I will keep this report very brief.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.860
As we have reflected before, this has been
a year of very significant operational and

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.230
financial challenges for the NHS as a whole,
and certainly the commissioning sector in

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:49.739
particular, but I am pleased to say, as the
report does, that barring any last-minute

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:54.890
accidents, we do appear to be on course to
deliver in full on our commitment to generate

00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:01.640
the £800 million of system reserve needed
to offset risks across the NHS. This policy

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:05.969
has clearly turned out to be very necessary.
Whether it turns out to be adequate to secure

00:01:05.969 --> 00:01:10.970
balance across the NHS will of course now
largely be determined by the final performance

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:15.820
of the provider sector, whose forecasts are
currently hovering in a tantalising range

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:19.570
around that £800 million mark.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:22.370
Just turning briefly to the numbers in the
report, I am pleased to be able to confirm

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:28.290
that the year-to-date position is now a marginal
underspend overall; that the core forecast

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.610
for the commissioning sector as a whole, before
the impact of releasing the £800 million

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:38.409
reserve, remains at break-even; and that the
risks to the delivery of that break-even position

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:43.740
have continued to reduce steadily from over
£0.5 billion at quarter one to nearly £300

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:49.640
million at the half-year point, £148 million
at quarter three, and now £34 million in

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:54.970
the report you have in front of you. We will
of course continue to work on mitigations

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:59.530
to close out the remainder of the risk, and
I have a reasonable line of sight to doing

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.010
so at this stage.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:05.420
The report sets out all the rest of the detail
on the position, but as there is nothing very

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:09.119
different from the last time we went through
all of this, I was not proposing today to

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:14.260
add further comment at this point. Clearly,
I stand ready to discuss any aspect of my

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.100
report with the Board.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:29.080
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Clearly, we have the commissioning
sector having very high levels of efficiency

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:34.781
ask this year in order to fund the £800 million
contingency reserve; that is reflected in,

00:02:34.781 --> 00:02:43.320
I think, the CCG numbers in terms of where
they stand. The balance this year has been

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:52.459
done by some very effective and good management,
and some difficult trade-offs within the central

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:57.230
management of the NHS England budgets, if
you like. I think it is also worth pointing

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:02.160
out specialised, which has done really well
in terms of its position and, again, good

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.340
management. The observation, Malcolm, is that
past performance is not a good predictor of

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:12.959
future success, and therefore, I think it
is really important that everyone recognise

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:22.349
that the same approach to managing the aggregate
budget for the year coming is just not as

00:03:22.349 --> 00:03:27.081
realistic, and therefore, we should be very
thoughtful going into the planning rounds,

00:03:27.081 --> 00:03:32.460
or the planning rounds going into the year,
that we have much less flexibility to be able

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:38.370
to pull levers in the centre of NHS England,
to be able to offset challenges elsewhere.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:43.550
Therefore, the importance of the integrity
of the operating and financial plans that

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:50.300
are being finalised at the moment, their realism
and the support in terms of actions that people

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:55.530
are taking to deliver outcomes, is really
critical, and the risk therefore is higher

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.080
than it has been.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.019
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Absolutely right, David.

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:06.290
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: I just wanted to say a word
about CCG audit chairs, because I think the

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:13.269
financial achievement that it looks like we
will get to this year is in no small part

00:04:13.269 --> 00:04:18.250
due to the really tough decisions and the
hard work that is being done at CCG level.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:22.960
As we know, CCG audit chairs are laypeople
who give up their time, take an enormous amount

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.650
of responsibility on our behalf, and we meet
with them a couple of times a year to try

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:34.470
and support them in their role. I think they
have done a fantastic job, and it would be

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.130
good to recognise that.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:42.129
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think we just need to first
of all commend the work that Paul and his

00:04:42.129 --> 00:04:46.130
team have done with the CCGs, including their
audit chairs. Secondly, to understand, actually,

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:51.360
there is still quite a lot of work to do to
close the position for this year, and each

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.409
year it gets tighter and harder, and games
may be played; it is really important for

00:04:56.409 --> 00:05:00.919
Paul and his team to be able to settle that
down and get a final figure as soon as we

00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:08.210
can. Then thirdly, to note David's point,
that next year will be worse. We say that

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:13.870
every year at this time, but we know, actually,
the coming year and two years are going to

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:22.020
be very tight financially. So, we commend
Paul for the work that you have done, and

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.169
we will need from an even earlier stage in
the coming financial year to be completely

00:05:26.169 --> 00:05:33.819
on top of the finances, but thank you again.

00:05:33.819 --> 00:05:54.569
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I suppose it is

00:05:54.569 --> 00:06:03.229
first worth noting that if the Civil Contingencies
Act kicks in, then NHS England takes control

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:09.279
of the NHS's resources, so that is why this
Board has taken an interest in this. In the

00:06:09.279 --> 00:06:16.240
past year, the accountable officer responsibility
is passed to me from Richard Barker, who was

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.729
acted into this role as an interim and has
now gone back to being the Regional Director

00:06:19.729 --> 00:06:26.490
in the North, so thanks to him. Anne Rainsberry,
who is the Regional Director for London, continues

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:34.389
to be our National Strategy and is the Incident
Director for National Incidents. Simon Weldon,

00:06:34.389 --> 00:06:39.110
who recently started as my National Director
for Ops and Delivery, has day-to-day responsibility,

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:46.099
and Stephen Groves is the National Head of
EPRR, so we have an infrastructure that runs

00:06:46.099 --> 00:06:54.099
through. There is a requirement for this Board
to receive reports at least annually, so you

00:06:54.099 --> 00:06:58.330
can note the progress that we have made in
the past year and receive assurance that the

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:05.649
NHS is focused on this issue and has the right
pieces in place. Then, subject to your agreement,

00:07:05.649 --> 00:07:11.800
this paper will be used to inform the NHSE
Annual Statement of Assurance to the Department

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:13.560
of Health.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:22.240
In the past year, we have conducted, as we
always do, assurance of the readiness of the

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:31.459
regional structures and the CCGs to deal with
an incident. We have done deep-dive reviews

00:07:31.459 --> 00:07:41.529
on business continuity and our preparedness
for a fuel disruption incident. We have participated

00:07:41.529 --> 00:07:47.529
in the Department of Health-led exercise Cygnus,
which was a three-day exercise on the potential

00:07:47.529 --> 00:07:56.309
impact of a pandemic flu outbreak. We have
responded to a number of real incidents, from

00:07:56.309 --> 00:08:01.870
the BMA junior doctor industrial action, where
we were working to protect the emergency care

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:09.550
pathway; to an e-coli outbreak in June, which
we worked on with Public Health England; to

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:17.020
the Croydon tram derailment in November where,
sadly, there were a number of deaths and some

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:24.020
injuries; and of course, last Wednesday's
attack on London; and we are currently going

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:28.180
through the exercise to learn what we need
to from that incident, in order that we are

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:37.270
better next time, although from the NHS's
point of view, it ran very smoothly and the

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:39.050
plans fell into place.

00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:46.010
Next year, alongside our other work, we will
be continuing to ensure that NHS England staff

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:50.410
are appropriately trained and that the plans
are in place for response where necessary.

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:58.680
We will be finalising work on the NHS response
to a mass casualty incident, and we will be

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:06.800
ensuring that we have the capacity and capability
in place to respond to major incidents. Subject

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:14.130
to questions, I hope you will accept this
report and the assurance that goes with it.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:20.810
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I just wanted to make sure
that everybody has had the opportunity to

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:28.420
see the EPRR headquarters, and to get a sense
of the organisation and the necessity of running

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:32.079
something the whole time that, for much of
the time, does not seem to be doing anything,

00:09:32.079 --> 00:09:37.240
but at a critical moment, can switch into
really high-level performance by gold commanders,

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:44.329
as you say, and provide a safe outcome. Last
Wednesday morning, I happened to be at the

00:09:44.329 --> 00:09:51.170
Transport for London control centre with exactly
the same capability, and with a very interesting

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:56.920
model in which the police are integrated with
their whole-service transport model, and again,

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:03.490
a command/control structure which allows them
to respond in parallel with the NHS in a major

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:06.350
incident, as tragically happened that same
afternoon.

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:12.029
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes. The thing you have
to deliver in these incidents is the integration

00:10:12.029 --> 00:10:19.279
between the public services. I was in the
control centre when we had the London bombs,

00:10:19.279 --> 00:10:25.379
and the link between ourselves, the fire services,
the police services and transport, is crucial

00:10:25.379 --> 00:10:30.360
to being able to create a co ordinated response
in order to be able to stop the trains so

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:36.519
that the paramedics can get onto the track,
get the electricity turned off. Those sorts

00:10:36.519 --> 00:10:41.040
of things are crucial integration. Some of
the things we have planned for, like a pandemic,

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:49.410
we are looking beyond just an England response
to what is a United Kingdom response. So,

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:55.459
those are the reasons that we run simulations
and do the training, and that we have the

00:10:55.459 --> 00:10:57.920
facilities on standby for when we need them.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:02.740
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: I just wondered, when we have
the kind of emergency that requires a contact

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:04.899
centre, who provides that, these days?

00:11:04.899 --> 00:11:10.000
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: That is provided through
111.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:15.670
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you for the report,
Matthew. We take assurance from that, and

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:20.089
this is the function of this regular report
to the Board, to reassure that we are actually

00:11:20.089 --> 00:11:23.600
doing the business. I think the thing that
should give us most assurance is the fact

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:27.580
that after each incident, we do a learning
process from it; we learn also from other

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:33.990
incidents - the Romanian nightclub fire that
you mention in your paper - to try to ensure

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:38.779
that our service is ready for action whenever
it is called upon, which by definition is

00:11:38.779 --> 00:11:42.810
completely unpredictable.

